---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import LineChart from "../components/LineChart.astro";
import StackedBars from "../components/StackedBars.astro";
import PanelLines from "../components/PanelLines.astro";
import PeriodTable from "../components/PeriodTable.astro";
import { series, latest } from "../lib/data.js";
import { num, period } from "../lib/format.js";
import { config } from "../lib/config.js";
import fs from "node:fs";
const repo = latest(series("repo_rate"));
const credit = latest(series("scb_credit_growth"));
const liquidity = latest(series("liquidity_surplus"));
const m3 = latest(series("m3_growth"));
const mpc = JSON.parse(fs.readFileSync("src/data/generated/mpc_decisions.json", "utf8"));
const lastMpc = mpc.rows[0];
const move = (bp) => bp === null ? "–" : bp === 0 ? "hold" : `${bp > 0 ? "+" : "−"}${Math.abs(bp)} bp`;
const creditContrib = JSON.parse(fs.readFileSync("src/data/generated/credit_contributions.json", "utf8"));
const trans = JSON.parse(fs.readFileSync("src/data/generated/transmission.json", "utf8"));
const panelJson = (id) => JSON.parse(fs.readFileSync(`src/data/generated/panels/${id}.json`, "utf8"));
const walrFresh = panelJson("walr_fresh"), wadtdrFresh = panelJson("wadtdr_fresh");
const benchmarks = panelJson("loan_benchmark_share"), walrSector = panelJson("walr_fresh_sector");
const groups = ["All scheduled commercial banks", "Public sector banks", "Private sector banks", "Foreign banks"];
const bp = (v) => (v === null || v === undefined ? "–" : `${v > 0 ? "+" : ""}${v}`);
const monthName = (d) => (d ? period(d, "M") : "now");
---
<Base current="money" title="Money & banking">
  <h1>Money &amp; banking</h1>
  <p class="lede">What is RBI's policy stance, where are money-market rates, and how fast are bank credit and deposits growing? The policy repo rate is {num(repo.value, 2)}% ({period(repo.period, "D")}); bank credit grew {num(credit.value, 1)}% on a year earlier in the fortnight to {period(credit.period, "D")}. The banking system's liquidity was in {liquidity.value >= 0 ? "surplus" : "deficit"} by ₹{num(Math.abs(liquidity.value), 2)} lakh crore on {period(liquidity.period, "D")}, and broad money (M3) grew {num(m3.value, 1)}% on a year earlier.</p>
  {mpc.next && <p class="chart-sub"><strong>Next MPC decision:</strong> {period(mpc.next.date, "D")} ({mpc.next.days} days). Last decision ({lastMpc.dates}): {move(lastMpc.change_bp)} to {num(lastMpc.rate, 2)}%, stance {lastMpc.stance}. <a href="/calendar/">Calendar →</a></p>}

  <LineChart title="Policy corridor and the call rate" subtitle="Per cent; the call rate (WACR) normally trades between the SDF floor and the MSF ceiling"
    lines={[{ id: "msf_rate", label: "MSF (ceiling)", step: true }, { id: "repo_rate", label: "Repo", step: true }, { id: "sdf_rate", label: "SDF (floor)", step: true }, { id: "wacr", label: "Call rate (WACR)" }]} years={1} />
  <p class="chart-sub">Week-ended values from RBI's Weekly Statistical Supplement, plus daily readings of RBI's home page since 28 Oct 2026.</p>

  <h2 id="decisions">MPC decisions</h2>
  <div class="scroll"><table class="data">
    <thead><tr><th>Meeting</th><th>Repo rate</th><th>Change</th><th>Vote on the rate</th><th>Stance</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      {mpc.rows.slice(0, 12).map((r) => <tr><td>{r.dates}</td><td>{num(r.rate, 2)}%</td><td>{move(r.change_bp)}</td>
        <td>{r.votes_for === null ? "–" : `${r.votes_for}–${r.votes_against}`}{r.size_split ? ` (${r.size_split})` : ""}</td><td>{r.stance}</td></tr>)}
    </tbody>
  </table></div>
  <details><summary>All {mpc.rows.length} meetings since October 2016</summary>
    <div class="scroll"><table class="data">
      <thead><tr><th>Meeting</th><th>Repo rate</th><th>Change</th><th>Vote</th><th>Stance</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {mpc.rows.slice(12).map((r) => <tr><td>{r.dates}</td><td>{num(r.rate, 2)}%</td><td>{move(r.change_bp)}</td>
          <td>{r.votes_for === null ? "–" : `${r.votes_for}–${r.votes_against}`}{r.size_split ? ` (${r.size_split})` : ""}</td><td>{r.stance}</td></tr>)}
      </tbody>
    </table></div>
  </details>
  <p class="chart-sub">From the resolution in each set of MPC minutes. Vote: members for and against the rate resolution; since 2025 the minutes also record the size of move each member voted for, and members who backed a different size are noted. The stance is as worded in the minutes; when the minutes don't restate it, the previous meeting's stance is shown. Source: RBI, minutes of the MPC meetings.</p>

  <h2 id="transmission">Transmission: from the repo rate to bank rates</h2>
  <div class="scroll"><table class="data">
    <thead><tr><th>Cycle</th><th>Repo rate</th><th>Deposit rate, new deposits</th><th>Deposit rate, outstanding</th><th>1-year MCLR (median)</th><th>Lending rate, new loans</th><th>Lending rate, outstanding loans</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      {trans.cycles.slice(0, 5).map((c) => <tr>
        <td>{c.kind}, {period(c.start, "M")} to {monthName(c.end)}</td><td>{bp(c.repo_bp)}</td>
        <td>{bp(c.wadtdr_fresh)}</td><td>{bp(c.wadtdr_outstanding)}</td><td>{bp(c.mclr_1y_median)}</td>
        <td>{bp(c.walr_fresh)}</td><td>{bp(c.walr_outstanding)}</td></tr>)}
    </tbody>
  </table></div>
  <p class="chart-sub">Change in basis points, all scheduled commercial banks, from the month before the cycle's first rate move to its end (the month before the first move the other way) or the latest month with data. A cycle can include meetings that held the rate. Rates on new loans and deposits move faster than those on the stock, which reprices only as loans and deposits roll over. These match RBI's own transmission table in the Monetary Policy Report (October 2026, Table II.7) for the same months. Source: RBI, Lending and Deposit Rates of Scheduled Commercial Banks (monthly); site calculations.</p>
  <div class="grid2">
    <PanelLines title="Lending rates on new loans" subtitle="Weighted average lending rate on fresh rupee loans, %" panel={walrFresh} entities={groups} years={4} source="RBI." />
    <PanelLines title="Deposit rates on new term deposits" subtitle="Weighted average domestic term deposit rate on fresh deposits, %" panel={wadtdrFresh} entities={groups} years={4} source="RBI." />
    <PanelLines title="Lending rates on new loans, by sector" subtitle="Weighted average lending rate on fresh rupee loans, all banks, %" panel={walrSector}
      entities={["Housing", "Vehicle", "MSMEs", "Industry (Large)", "Agriculture", "Other Personal Loans"]} years={4} source="RBI." />
    <PanelLines title="How floating-rate loans are priced" subtitle="Share of outstanding floating-rate loans by benchmark, all banks, %; EBLR loans are mostly linked to the repo rate and reprice with it"
      panel={benchmarks} entities={["EBLR", "MCLR", "Base Rate", "Others"]} years={8} source="RBI." />
  </div>

  <h2 id="liquidity">Liquidity</h2>
  <LineChart title="Banking system liquidity" subtitle="₹ lakh crore, daily; net absorption (+) or injection (−) by RBI across all its operations. Above zero, banks hold surplus funds"
    lines={[{ id: "liquidity_surplus", label: "Surplus (+) / deficit (−)" }]} reference={{ target: 0 }} years={2} />
  <p class="chart-sub">RBI prints this as net injection (+) / absorption (−); the sign is flipped here so that a surplus is positive. Includes LAF repo and reverse repo (fixed and variable rate), MSF, SDF, standing liquidity facilities and outright open-market operations. Source: RBI, Weekly Statistical Supplement, table 8.</p>

  <h2 id="credit">Bank credit and deposits</h2>
  <div class="grid2">
    <LineChart title="Bank credit and deposit growth, as published" subtitle="Scheduled commercial banks, % change on a year earlier"
      lines={[{ id: "scb_credit_growth", label: "Credit" }, { id: "scb_deposits_growth", label: "Deposits" }]} years={1} />
    <LineChart title="Outstanding" subtitle="₹ lakh crore, scheduled commercial banks"
      lines={[{ id: "scb_deposits", label: "Deposits" }, { id: "scb_credit", label: "Credit" }]} years={1} />
  </div>
  <p class="chart-sub">RBI notes that these data include the effect of a non-bank merging into a bank, which lifts year-on-year growth; see the note in the WSS table.</p>
  <div class="grid2">
    <LineChart title="Credit–deposit ratio" subtitle="Bank credit as % of aggregate deposits, scheduled commercial banks, fortnightly (site calculation from the two RBI series)"
      lines={[{ id: "credit_deposit_ratio", label: "Credit–deposit ratio" }]} years={1} />
    <LineChart title="Credit–deposit gap" subtitle="₹ lakh crore: rise in bank credit over the past year minus rise in deposits; above zero, lending outpaced deposits"
      lines={[{ id: "credit_deposit_gap", label: "Gap" }]} reference={{ target: 0 }} years={1} />
  </div>
  <p class="chart-sub">When the gap is positive, banks are lending out more new money than they take in as deposits and must fund the difference by borrowing, issuing certificates of deposit or selling investments; that is when CD rates tend to rise. Site calculation from RBI's year-on-year changes in the Weekly Statistical Supplement (table 4).</p>

  <h2 id="sectoral-credit">Who is borrowing: credit by sector</h2>
  <div class="grid2">
    <LineChart title="Bank credit growth by sector" subtitle="% change on a year earlier, as published"
      lines={[{ id: "credit_agri_growth", label: "Agriculture" }, { id: "credit_industry_growth", label: "Industry" }, { id: "credit_services_growth", label: "Services" }, { id: "credit_personal_growth", label: "Personal loans" }]} years={2} />
    <LineChart title="Personal loans by type" subtitle="% change on a year earlier, as published"
      lines={[{ id: "credit_housing_growth", label: "Housing" }, { id: "credit_vehicle_growth", label: "Vehicle" }, { id: "credit_cards_growth", label: "Credit cards" }]} years={2} />
  </div>
  <div class="grid2">
    <StackedBars title="What is driving credit growth" subtitle="Percentage points of y/y growth in credit to the four main sectors; the line is their total growth"
      rows={creditContrib.credit_contrib_sectors.rows} total={creditContrib.credit_contrib_sectors.total} yLabel="pp"
      source="RBI, sectoral deployment of bank credit; site calculations." />
    <StackedBars title="What is driving personal loans" subtitle="Percentage points of y/y growth in personal loans; the line is their growth as published"
      rows={creditContrib.credit_contrib_personal.rows} total={creditContrib.credit_contrib_personal.total} yLabel="pp"
      source="RBI, sectoral deployment of bank credit; site calculations." />
  </div>
  <details><summary>Industry by firm size, and services by type</summary>
    <div class="grid2">
      <StackedBars title="Credit to industry" subtitle="Percentage points of y/y growth, by firm size"
        rows={creditContrib.credit_contrib_industry.rows} total={creditContrib.credit_contrib_industry.total} yLabel="pp"
        source="RBI; site calculations." />
      <StackedBars title="Credit to services" subtitle="Percentage points of y/y growth"
        rows={creditContrib.credit_contrib_services.rows} total={creditContrib.credit_contrib_services.total} yLabel="pp"
        source="RBI; site calculations." />
    </div>
  </details>
  <p class="chart-sub">Each bar splits a growth rate into the part due to each kind of borrower: the rise in that borrower's loans over the year as a share of the total a year earlier. The parts add up to the growth rate (and match RBI's published growth for personal loans, industry and services). The sector chart covers the four main sectors, which make up about 95% of non-food credit; RBI reports total non-food credit on a different date. Amounts from RBI's monthly Statement 1; the month, a year earlier and two years earlier come from each release.</p>
  <PeriodTable heading="% y/y, last reporting day of the month" n={6} rows={[
    { id: "credit_nonfood_growth", label: "Non-food credit" },
    { id: "credit_agri_growth", indent: true }, { id: "credit_industry_growth", indent: true }, { id: "credit_services_growth", indent: true },
    { id: "credit_nbfc_growth", label: "  of which NBFCs", indent: true }, { id: "credit_personal_growth", indent: true },
    { id: "credit_housing_growth", label: "  Housing", indent: true }, { id: "credit_vehicle_growth", label: "  Vehicle", indent: true },
    { id: "credit_gold_growth", label: "  Gold loans", indent: true }, { id: "credit_cards_growth", label: "  Credit cards", indent: true },
  ]} freq="M" />
  <p class="chart-sub">RBI's monthly Sectoral Deployment of Bank Credit (Statement 1), growth rates as published. Gold loans are left out of the chart because their growth is far larger than the rest; they are in the table. Each release also restates the year-earlier rate.</p>

  <h2 id="money-supply">Money supply</h2>
  <div class="grid2">
    <LineChart title="Broad money (M3) and reserve money growth" subtitle="% change on a year earlier, as published"
      lines={[{ id: "m3_growth", label: "M3" }, { id: "reserve_money_growth", label: "Reserve money" }, { id: "currency_public_growth", label: "Currency with the public" }]} years={2} />
    <LineChart title="Outstanding" subtitle="₹ lakh crore"
      lines={[{ id: "m3", label: "M3" }]} years={2} />
  </div>
  <p class="chart-sub">Fortnightly. M3 includes the effect of a non-bank merging into a bank from July 2023 (RBI's note). Source: RBI, Weekly Statistical Supplement, tables 6 and 7.</p>

  <h2 id="paper">Commercial paper and certificates of deposit</h2>
  <div class="grid2">
    <LineChart title="Rates on new issues" subtitle="Per cent; the low and high ends of the range at which paper was issued in each fortnight"
      lines={[{ id: "cd_rate_low", label: "CDs, low" }, { id: "cd_rate_high", label: "CDs, high" }, { id: "cp_rate_low", label: "CP, low" }, { id: "cp_rate_high", label: "CP, high" }]} years={2} />
    <LineChart title="Outstanding" subtitle="₹ lakh crore"
      lines={[{ id: "cp_outstanding", label: "Commercial paper" }, { id: "cd_outstanding", label: "Certificates of deposit" }]} years={2} />
  </div>
  <p class="chart-sub">Certificates of deposit are short-term borrowing by banks; commercial paper is short-term borrowing by companies and non-banks. RBI prints a range of rates for each fortnight, not an average, so the high end can jump with a single small issue by a weaker borrower. Source: RBI, Weekly Statistical Supplement, tables 10 and 11.</p>

  <h2 id="rates-table">Rates, recent weeks</h2>
  <PeriodTable heading="Per cent, week ended" n={6} digits={2} rows={[
    { id: "repo_rate" }, { id: "sdf_rate", indent: true }, { id: "msf_rate", indent: true },
    { id: "crr" }, { id: "slr" }, { id: "wacr" },
    { id: "tbill_91d_weekly" }, { id: "tbill_182d_weekly" }, { id: "tbill_364d_weekly" }, { id: "gsec_10y_par_fbil" },
  ]} freq="D" />

</Base>
